# Véridian E-commerce - AI Development Rules

## Project Context
Full-stack premium e-commerce platform built with React 19, TypeScript, Supabase, and Stripe.
Target: High-end retail stores requiring modern, scalable, secure solution with AI recommendations.
Multi-role system: customer, staff, admin, kiosk.

## Tech Stack

### Frontend
- React 19.0.1 with TypeScript 5.8.2
- Vite 6.2.3 for build and dev server
- Tailwind CSS 4.1.14 for styling
- Framer Motion 12.23.24 for animations
- Zustand 5.0.13 for state management
- React Router 7.15.1 for routing
- Lucide React 0.546.0 for icons

### Backend
- Express 4.21.2 for HTTP/WebSocket server
- Supabase 2.106.2 (PostgreSQL + Auth + Storage)
- Stripe API for payments
- Google Gemini 2.4.0 for AI recommendations

### Dev Tools
- tsx for TypeScript execution
- esbuild for server bundling
- ESLint + TypeScript compiler for linting

## Code Style & Conventions

### TypeScript
- Use strict mode (`"strict": true` in tsconfig.json)
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for object shapes
- Use `type` for unions, intersections, primitives
- Always define return types for functions
- Use `const` by default, `let` only when reassignment needed
- Never use `any`, prefer `unknown` or proper typing
- Use optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`)

### React
- Use functional components only, no class components
- Prefer named exports over default exports for components
- Use TypeScript for props, never PropTypes
- Destructure props in function signature
- Use custom hooks for reusable logic
- Prefix custom hooks with `use` (e.g., `usePWA`)
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Use useCallback for functions passed as props
- Use useMemo for expensive computations

### Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (e.g., `ProductCard.tsx`)
- Files: PascalCase for components, camelCase for utilities
- Functions: camelCase (e.g., `handleSubmit`)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `API_URL`)
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase (e.g., `User`, `CartItem`)
- CSS classes: kebab-case via Tailwind utilities

### File Organization
- One component per file
- Co-locate related files (component + styles + tests)
- Group by feature, not by type
- Keep files under 300 lines, split if larger
- Index files for clean imports

## Architecture Patterns

### Component Structure
```typescript
// 1. Imports (React, libraries, local)
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { motion } from 'motion/react';
import { Button } from './ui/Button';

// 2. Types/Interfaces
interface Props {
  title: string;
  onSubmit: () => void;
}

// 3. Component
export function MyComponent({ title, onSubmit }: Props) {
  // 4. Hooks (state, effects, custom hooks)
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
  
  // 5. Event handlers
  const handleClick = () => {
    setIsOpen(true);
    onSubmit();
  };
  
  // 6. Render
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <Button onClick={handleClick}>Submit</Button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### State Management
- Use Zustand for global state
- Use React state for local UI state
- Never duplicate state between Zustand and React
- Keep Zustand store flat, avoid deep nesting
- Use selectors to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- Persist cart and favorites in localStorage

### Data Fetching
- Use Supabase client for all DB operations
- Always handle loading and error states
- Use optimistic updates for better UX
- Implement retry logic for failed requests
- Cache responses when appropriate

### Error Handling
- Always wrap async operations in try/catch
- Use toast notifications for user-facing errors
- Log errors to console in development
- Use Sentry in production (when implemented)
- Provide fallback UI for error states

## Best Practices

### Performance
- Lazy load routes with React.lazy()
- Use code splitting for large components
- Optimize images (WebP, lazy loading, blur placeholder)
- Debounce search and filter inputs
- Use virtual scrolling for long lists
- Minimize bundle size (check with `npm run build`)
- Use React DevTools Profiler to identify bottlenecks

### Security
- Never expose secrets in client code
- Use environment variables for all API keys
- Validate all inputs on server side
- Use Supabase RLS for database security
- Sanitize user inputs to prevent XSS
- Use HTTPS only in production
- Implement rate limiting on API endpoints
- Use Stripe for PCI-compliant payments

### Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Add ARIA labels where needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Test with screen readers
- Maintain color contrast ratios (WCAG AA)
- Support reduced motion preferences
- Add focus indicators for interactive elements

### SEO
- Use React Helmet for meta tags
- Generate sitemap.xml dynamically
- Use semantic HTML (h1, h2, article, etc.)
- Add structured data (Schema.org)
- Optimize images with alt text
- Use descriptive URLs

### Testing
- Write unit tests for business logic
- Test edge cases and error scenarios
- Mock external dependencies (Supabase, Stripe)
- Use TypeScript to catch type errors
- Run `npm run lint` before committing

## Common Workflows

### Adding a New Component
1. Create file in `src/components/` with PascalCase name
2. Define TypeScript interface for props
3. Implement component with proper typing
4. Add to index.ts for clean imports
5. Use in parent component

### Adding a New Page
1. Create file in `src/pages/` with PascalCase name
2. Add route in `App.tsx`
3. Add to lazy loading if needed
4. Implement SEO meta tags
5. Add to navigation if needed

### Adding a New API Endpoint
1. Add route in `server.ts`
2. Implement handler function
3. Add error handling
4. Test with Postman/Insomnia
5. Document in API_DOCS.md

### Database Migration
1. Create SQL file in `supabase/migrations/`
2. Use format: `YYYYMMDD_description.sql`
3. Make migration additive (no DROP TABLE)
4. Test locally first
5. Run in Supabase SQL Editor
6. Update DB_SCHEMA.md

### Adding a Zustand Action
1. Define action in store interface
2. Implement action in store
3. Add TypeScript types
4. Use in component with selector
5. Test state updates

## Preferred Dependencies

### UI Components
- Use Lucide React for icons (not Font Awesome)
- Use Framer Motion for animations (not react-spring)
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling (no CSS-in-JS)
- Use React Hot Toast for notifications (not react-toastify)

### Forms
- Use React Hook Form for complex forms
- Use Zod for validation schemas
- Use native HTML5 validation when possible

### Date/Time
- Use date-fns for date manipulation (not moment.js)
- Use native Intl API for formatting when possible

### HTTP Requests
- Use Supabase client for DB (not axios)
- Use native fetch for external APIs
- Use Stripe SDK for payments

## Anti-patterns to Avoid

### React
- Never mutate state directly, use setState
- Never use index as key in lists
- Never define components inside other components
- Never use inline functions in JSX (use useCallback)
- Never forget to cleanup effects (return cleanup function)
- Never use useEffect for derived state (use useMemo)

### TypeScript
- Never use `any` type
- Never use `as` casting unless absolutely necessary
- Never ignore TypeScript errors with `@ts-ignore`
- Never use non-null assertion (`!`) without checking

### Performance
- Never fetch data in render function
- Never create objects/arrays in render without memoization
- Never use large images without optimization
- Never block main thread with heavy computations

### Security
- Never store secrets in code or git
- Never trust client-side validation alone
- Never expose admin endpoints without auth
- Never use eval() or dangerouslySetInnerHTML

### Database
- Never query database from client without RLS
- Never use SELECT * in production
- Never forget to add indexes on foreign keys
- Never delete data without backup

## Documentation Rules

### Code Comments
- Write comments for complex logic only
- Use JSDoc for public functions
- Explain "why", not "what"
- Keep comments up to date
- Remove commented-out code

### Commit Messages
- Use Conventional Commits format
- Format: `type(scope): description`
- Types: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore
- Example: `feat(checkout): add Stripe payment integration`
- Keep first line under 72 characters
- Add body for complex changes

### Pull Requests
- Write clear title and description
- Reference related issues
- Add screenshots for UI changes
- Request review from team
- Ensure CI passes before merging

### API Documentation
- Document all endpoints in API_DOCS.md
- Include request/response examples
- Document error codes
- Keep documentation in sync with code

## Supabase Specific

### RLS Policies
- Always enable RLS on new tables
- Test policies with different user roles
- Use `auth.uid()` for user-specific data
- Use role checks for admin operations
- Never bypass RLS in client code

### Queries
- Use `.select()` with specific columns
- Use `.single()` for single row queries
- Use `.maybeSingle()` when row might not exist
- Handle errors with try/catch
- Use `.order()` for sorted results

### Real-time
- Subscribe to changes for live updates
- Unsubscribe in cleanup function
- Handle connection errors gracefully
- Use channels for specific tables

## Stripe Specific

### Payments
- Always use PaymentIntents (not Charges)
- Validate webhook signatures
- Use idempotency keys for retries
- Handle all payment states (succeeded, failed, pending)
- Store payment metadata for reconciliation

### Security
- Never log full card numbers
- Use Stripe.js for card input (PCI compliance)
- Validate amounts on server side
- Use webhook secrets for verification

## AI/Gemini Specific

### Prompts
- Be specific and clear in prompts
- Provide context and examples
- Handle API errors gracefully
- Cache responses when appropriate
- Respect rate limits

### Recommendations
- Personalize based on user history
- Fall back to popular items if no data
- A/B test recommendation algorithms
- Track recommendation performance

## Development Workflow

### Before Starting Work
1. Pull latest changes from main
2. Create feature branch: `feature/description`
3. Run `npm install` if package.json changed
4. Run `npm run dev` to start dev server

### During Development
1. Write code following these rules
2. Test in browser frequently
3. Check TypeScript errors: `npm run lint`
4. Commit often with clear messages
5. Push to remote branch regularly

### Before Committing
1. Run `npm run lint` (no errors)
2. Test all changed functionality
3. Check for console errors/warnings
4. Review your own code
5. Write clear commit message

### Before Pull Request
1. Rebase on latest main
2. Resolve conflicts if any
3. Run full build: `npm run build`
4. Test production build locally
5. Write PR description

## Emergency Procedures

### Production Bug
1. Assess severity (critical/high/medium/low)
2. Create hotfix branch from main
3. Fix bug with minimal changes
4. Test thoroughly
5. Deploy immediately if critical
6. Create post-mortem document

### Database Issue
1. Never run destructive queries in production
2. Always backup before migrations
3. Test migrations in staging first
4. Have rollback plan ready
5. Monitor after deployment

### Security Incident
1. Assess impact immediately
2. Rotate compromised credentials
3. Patch vulnerability
4. Notify affected users if needed
5. Document incident and response

## Performance Targets

- Lighthouse Score: > 90
- First Contentful Paint: < 1.5s
- Time to Interactive: < 3s
- Bundle Size: < 500KB (gzipped)
- API Response Time: < 200ms (p95)
- Database Query Time: < 100ms (p95)

## Code Review Checklist

- [ ] Code follows style guide
- [ ] TypeScript types are correct
- [ ] No console.log in production code
- [ ] Error handling is present
- [ ] Loading states are handled
- [ ] Accessibility is considered
- [ ] Performance is optimized
- [ ] Security is not compromised
- [ ] Tests pass (if applicable)
- [ ] Documentation is updated

